# The build context for `storefront/Dockerfile`, which is the repository root.
#
# BuildKit reads this file because it is named after the Dockerfile and sits
# beside it; a plain `storefront/.dockerignore` would be read only if the
# context were `storefront/`, which it cannot be — the application imports
# `content/` and `design/` by relative path and its dependency tree comes from
# the workspace lockfile at the repository root. `scripts/images.test.ts`
# asserts the name.

# Build output and dependency trees. Every one of these is produced inside the
# image from the lockfile; a copy from the developer's machine would silently
# replace it with whatever that machine happens to have — and a stale `.next`
# copied into the context is the classic way a build-time value from somebody's
# workstation ends up in a published image.
**/node_modules
**/.next
**/.next-test
**/.medusa
**/coverage
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# Version control, CI definitions and hooks. None of it is application code.
.git
.gitignore
.githooks
.github

# Tests, fixtures and the committed screenshot baselines — 5.9 MB of PNGs that
# exist to be compared in CI, never to be served.
**/tests
**/*.test.ts
**/*.test.tsx
**/*.spec.ts
**/*.spec.tsx
**/vitest.config.ts
**/vitest.config.mts
**/playwright.config.ts

# Design masters. `storefront/public/` carries web derivatives only, held to a
# byte ceiling by `storefront/tests/no-live-hostname.test.ts`; a master that
# ever does get committed by mistake must not also reach a published image.
**/*.psd
**/*.ai
**/*.sketch
**/*.fig
**/*.xcf
**/*.afdesign
**/*.afphoto
**/*.tif
**/*.tiff

# Prose, plans and local environment files.
**/*.md
docs
**/.env
**/.env.*

# The other workspace. Its `package.json` is re-included because npm reads
# every workspace manifest before it resolves anything, even when installing
# only this one.
backend
!backend/package.json
